WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.900
Choosing someone to take over a role&nbsp;
from another actor is never easy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:08.220
especially when the original actor drew praise&nbsp;
for their take on a character. In the case of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:12.540
these casting switcheroos, the change was&nbsp;
just too drastic for audiences to endure.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:16.860
When Ridley Scott signed on to direct&nbsp;
the Silence of the Lambs sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:22.560
he did so without his Clarice Starling in place.&nbsp;
Anthony Hopkins had already negotiated a return to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.060
the role of Hannibal Lecter, but Jodie Foster&nbsp;
didn't share his enthusiasm for the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:31.440
chapter in the serial killer's story, despite&nbsp;
winning an Oscar for the role in the first film.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:37.080
She later told Total Film, "The official reason I&nbsp;
didn't do Hannibal is I was doing another movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:42.120
Flora Plum. So I get to say, in a nice,&nbsp;
dignified way, that I wasn't available when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:46.680
that movie was being shot. But Clarice meant&nbsp;
so much to [Lambs director Jonathan Demme]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.160
and I … there was no way that either&nbsp;
of us could really trample on her."

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:56.801
"Now that's really interesting,&nbsp;
Clarice. I'm really pressed for time."

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:01.440
Several big name actresses were considered to&nbsp;
take her place, and the part ultimately went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:06.060
to Julianne Moore. While both Scott and Hopkins&nbsp;
were happy with the choice, audiences weren't as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:11.340
convinced. Moore was inevitably compared to Foster&nbsp;
and couldn't measure up to her predecessor's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:16.200
screen savvy nor rekindle the creepy connection&nbsp;
Clarice had shared with Hopkins in the first film.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.820
Jodie Foster offered her own diplomatic&nbsp;
but shady take on the film, saying, simply,

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:22.680
"I saw Hannibal. I won't comment."

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:28.680
The Mummy was a surprise smash for Universal,&nbsp;
posting global receipts of $415 million and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:33.840
landing on the top 10 highest-grossing films&nbsp;
of 1999. It launched Brendan Fraser's career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.400
as a Hollywood action hero and introduced the&nbsp;
world to a stunning new leading lady in Rachel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.080
Weisz. The pair made a compelling onscreen couple&nbsp;
and reprised their roles in The Mummy Returns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:46.200
which managed to bring in a nice&nbsp;
haul from the box office as well.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.460
But by the time the third film came&nbsp;
around, Weisz was suffering from some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:54.660
serious Mummy fatigue and stepped aside.&nbsp;
Weisz was rumored to call the script for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:59.121
the Mummy 3 "crap," so the studio turned&nbsp;
to Maria Bello to fill in for the role.

00:01:59.121 --> 00:02:01.142
"That should give us a good advantage."

00:02:01.142 --> 00:02:05.280
Unfortunately for Bello, her turn in the role&nbsp;
was criticized for lacking the charm of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:09.734
original and the film failed to recoup its&nbsp;
production costs in the domestic box office.

00:02:09.734 --> 00:02:12.960
"Hahhahaa, why am I laughing?"

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:17.700
The '90s marked a moment of resurgence for&nbsp;
Disney's animated film collection, with films like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:22.320
Beauty and the Beast and The Lion King offering a&nbsp;
new generation of children some cartoon movies to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:27.720
sing along to. One of Disney's most beloved films&nbsp;
of the era was Aladdin, an Oscar-winning take on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:32.172
the Arabic folk tale about the street thief who&nbsp;
shed his rags and won the heart of a princess.

00:02:32.172 --> 00:02:36.300
Aladdin was packed full of great musical&nbsp;
numbers, but the main reason it became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:39.720
such a smash was the input of the&nbsp;
late Robin Williams as the Genie.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.040
"It's all part and parcel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:47.000
the whole Genie gig. Phenomenal cosmic&nbsp;
power … itty bitty living space."

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:51.000
The actor confirmed that he wouldn't be&nbsp;
lending his voice to the direct-to-video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.680
sequel The Return of Jafar after an alleged&nbsp;
row with Disney over its marketing tactics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.660
so the search for a new Genie began.

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:03.120
Dan Castellaneta, the legendary voice of Homer&nbsp;
Simpson, was ultimately chosen to voice the big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.680
blue guy, but the Simpsons star just couldn't&nbsp;
measure up to Williams' frenetic performance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:13.440
The quality of the animation didn't help matters,&nbsp;
but Castellaneta lacked the unpredictability that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:18.188
Williams brought to the Genie, essentially doing&nbsp;
a watered-down impression of his predecessor.

00:03:18.188 --> 00:03:19.080
"D'oh!"

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:24.900
There are five films based on Tom Clancy's Jack&nbsp;
Ryan books, but only one actor has played the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:30.120
protagonist twice. Eyebrows were raised when Alec&nbsp;
Baldwin was given the boot after he successfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:34.200
debuted Ryan on-screen in The Hunt for the&nbsp;
Red October. Baldwin later implied that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:39.330
studio had gone behind his back and entered into&nbsp;
negotiations with Ford to take the role of Ryan.

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:41.520
"Well that was a guess, but it seemed logical."

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.700
Even so, Ford's performance in Patriot Games and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:47.580
Clear and Present Danger became&nbsp;
definitive for the character.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:51.540
Eight years later, another actor stepped&nbsp;
in to bring a much younger version of Jack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:55.560
Ryan to the big screen. This time, it&nbsp;
was Ben Affleck who stepped in for The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:58.920
Sum of All Fears. His performance&nbsp;
in the role was met with disdain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:02.580
however, and the Jack Ryan franchise&nbsp;
never really recovered after that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:06.866
despite an attempt to reboot it with Chris&nbsp;
Pine in the lead more than a decade later.

00:04:06.866 --> 00:04:11.220
Following in the footsteps of Linda Hamilton&nbsp;
as Sarah Connor in the Terminator franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:15.660
was never going to be easy for any actress.&nbsp;
Hamilton gave a performance so layered and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:18.840
compelling that even the most talented&nbsp;
performers would have struggled to do the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:23.100
character justice. The first to give it a shot&nbsp;
was Lena Headey, who was cast in the role in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:28.380
the short-lived TV series Terminator: The Sarah&nbsp;
Connor Chronicles. Fox cancelled the show after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:33.854
two seasons citing its high cost as a reason,&nbsp;
but Headey's Game of Thrones colleague Emilia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.854 --> 00:04:38.034
Clarke was willing to give it another go on&nbsp;
the silver screen for Terminator: Genisys.

00:04:38.034 --> 00:04:43.620
The film was a box office dud and was heavily&nbsp;
criticized for using the old time travel chestnut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:47.700
to rewrite the beginning of the Terminator&nbsp;
story to suit the filmmakers' purposes. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:53.580
main problem wasn't the infuriatingly muddled&nbsp;
timeline, though. It was the casting. As much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:58.320
as TV lovers might dig Clarke's portrayal&nbsp;
of Daenerys Targaryen in Game of Thrones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:02.940
the film crowd was decidedly not&nbsp;
wowed by her run as Sarah Connor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:06.240
where her line deliveries packed a&nbsp;
lot less punch than the original.

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.220
"Come with me if you want to live."

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:09.435
Pass.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:15.240
National Lampoon's Van Wilder isn't going to be&nbsp;
preserved in the Library of Congress National&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:20.340
Film Registry anytime soon. Yet for all its&nbsp;
failings, fans of a certain age remember this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:25.320
movie fondly. This was a time when frat humor&nbsp;
was at its peak, and sitting down to watch a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:30.360
comedy usually meant turning your brain off for&nbsp;
90 minutes. Van Wilder might not have been the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:35.040
most cerebral comedy of all time, but Ryan&nbsp;
Reynolds still delivered as the fast-talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:39.540
cool guy who threw the best parties and was&nbsp;
a softie underneath all those crazy antics.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:42.960
After the Kal Penn-led sequel&nbsp;
was destroyed by critics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:47.280
the producers decided their next move&nbsp;
would be a prequel featuring a younger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.380
version of Van Wilder. Reynolds refused&nbsp;
to return to the role, so they brought in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:54.480
Mean Girls' Jonathan Bennett for Van Wilder:&nbsp;
Freshman Year — which was a terrible mistake.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:58.734
"I have never seen such a&nbsp;
sorry display in all my life."

00:05:58.734 --> 00:06:02.160
The actor couldn't come close to&nbsp;
the charisma of his predecessor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:05.160
produced a feature-length failed&nbsp;
impression effort as a result.

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:09.720
Major League was a surprise grand slam&nbsp;
for Paramount Pictures and produced two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:14.160
increasingly terrible sequels. And while&nbsp;
Charlie Sheen's Wild Thing came back for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:17.640
a second round at the mound in Major&nbsp;
League II, there was another standout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:21.240
actor from the first film who refused to&nbsp;
run the bases of this franchise again.

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:26.340
Wesley Snipes, whose charming swagger as the&nbsp;
speedy base thief was a homerun in the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:30.540
movie, had no interest in returning to the&nbsp;
dugout for the second movie. At the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:33.900
he was well on his way to becoming&nbsp;
a Hollywood MVP in his own right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:38.280
thanks to movies like White Men Can't Jump&nbsp;
and Demolition Man, so he pretty much priced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:41.820
himself out of the second movie. With the&nbsp;
Cleveland Indians down a man on the roster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:46.920
the filmmakers turned to relative newcomer Omar&nbsp;
Epps to sub in as pinch runner for the project.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:50.100
Unfortunately, Epps didn't&nbsp;
have a strong background in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:53.760
comedy and his performance was the&nbsp;
cinematic equivalent of a foul ball.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:57.660
"Read some of your reviews man. One guy&nbsp;
said Willie Mays Hayes couldn't play dead."

00:06:57.660 --> 00:06:59.935
Just a bit outside.

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:04.680
The Prisoner of Azkaban might still be&nbsp;
a favorite among some Harry Potter fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:09.420
thanks to the arrival of Sirius Black and&nbsp;
that wild whomping willow. But the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:14.040
remains that it's the lowest grossing movie&nbsp;
in the franchise, and for many, the subdued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:19.380
enthusiasm seems to stem from the unfortunate&nbsp;
arrival of a new Hogwarts headmaster in the film.

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:24.240
Richard Harris' take on Albus Dumbledore in&nbsp;
the first two movies had become definitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:28.080
for Potter fans. So when the actor died&nbsp;
after a battle with Hodgkin's disease,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:32.700
the filmmakers had to turn to another actor&nbsp;
to step into the beloved professor's cloak.

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:36.780
Michael Gambon seemed like a fine choice&nbsp;
to conjure up for the franchise, but his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:41.280
performance in his first run as Dumbledore was&nbsp;
stunted by the fact that he never read the books.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:46.920
Rather than resuming Harris' spellbindingly&nbsp;
calm and wise demeanor, Gambon delivered a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:51.000
much more stern and aggressive version of&nbsp;
the character for his first few outings.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:53.414
"Harry! Did you put your&nbsp;
name in the Goblet of Fire?"

00:07:53.414 --> 00:07:54.616
"No sir!"

00:07:54.616 --> 00:07:56.443
"Did you ask one of the older&nbsp;
students to do it for you?"

00:07:56.443 --> 00:07:58.792
"No sir!"
"You’re absolutely sure?"

00:07:58.792 --> 00:07:59.332
"Yes."

00:07:59.332 --> 00:08:04.080
Gambon would later summon up some sort of&nbsp;
chill pill that saved the role from being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:08.700
ruined for children across the globe, but these&nbsp;
moments of mismatching between the beloved book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:12.600
character and the screen interpretation&nbsp;
still cost the movies many house points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:17.520
in the eyes of the true Potter fanatics. 
 
Halle Berry taking over Michelle Pfeiffer's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:21.960
role in Catwoman is perhaps the most infamous&nbsp;
example of recasting going terribly wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:27.300
DC's leather-clad heroine has appeared in&nbsp;
numerous Batman adaptations over the years, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:32.580
Pfeiffer's psychotically sexy performance in Tim&nbsp;
Burton's Batman Returns remains the cat's meow.

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:38.760
"I don't know about you Miss&nbsp;
Kitty but I feel so much yummier."

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:42.300
The actress initially intended&nbsp;
to reprise the role in the solo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:44.880
spinoff. But after years of development woes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:48.780
her patience reached its limit and she&nbsp;
decided to concentrate on other commitments.

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:53.340
After a rigorous recruiting process,&nbsp;
Berry was ultimately chosen to climb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:57.540
into the catsuit — and what a mess it all&nbsp;
was. The film was a complete disaster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:01.800
bombing hard at the box office while&nbsp;
cleaning up at the Razzies. Berry took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:06.060
her Worst Actress win in good humor and even&nbsp;
turned up to accept her award in person.

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:10.620
Another error of judgment from the Batman&nbsp;
universe occurred when Michael Keaton handed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:15.660
over the keys for Wayne Manor to Val Kilmer.&nbsp;
Keaton had portrayed the role in 1989's Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:21.060
and its sequel Batman Returns, but he refused to&nbsp;
reprise the role in a third film after reading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:25.800
the screenplay. When pressed for details, Keaton&nbsp;
later said that the script he was shown "sucked"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:29.880
and that "it was just awful," pointing the&nbsp;
blame for his departure squarely at the writers.

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:34.260
Val Kilmer clearly didn't share Keaton's&nbsp;
opinion. The Tombstone star accepted the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:38.940
role and went into the film knowing he'd have&nbsp;
to contend with a wide cast of wacky villains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:43.260
as well as a new director. Joel Schumacher&nbsp;
ultimately shouldered a lot of the blame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:46.920
for the badness of Batman Forever, though&nbsp;
his leading man didn't escape criticism.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:48.360
"What am I?"

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:54.732
"Please, you're as blind as a bat."

00:09:54.732 --> 00:09:59.400
He was quickly replaced with George Clooney&nbsp;
in Batman &amp; Robin. The good news for Kilmer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:03.420
is that Clooney's turn as the The Dark&nbsp;
Knight wasn't any more loved than his own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:06.300
and soon everyone forgot about&nbsp;
Kilmer's turn in the batmobile.

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:11.820
"Forget it, take it off. It’s Val Kilmer."

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:15.120
"Are you an actor?"

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:19.500
Roger Moore and Sean Connery each&nbsp;
made seven appearances as James Bond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:25.020
though many 007 fans will tell you that&nbsp;
Connery is the only Bond. A CBS News poll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:29.220
concluded that 51 percent of Americans&nbsp;
chose Connery as their favorite Bond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:35.220
with Pierce Brosnan coming in a distant second.&nbsp;
George Lazenby, the man who replaced Connery in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:40.620
the role for 1969's On Her Majesty's Secret&nbsp;
Service, received just 1 percent of the vote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:44.580
which tells you everything you need to know&nbsp;
about the gulf between these two actors.

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:48.120
While critical opinion toward the&nbsp;
film itself has softened over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:53.460
Lazenby's performance is still a bone of&nbsp;
contention — inevitably compared to those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:58.500
delivered by Connery in the franchise's five&nbsp;
previous entries. The Chicago Reader said that,

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:02.040
"George Lazenby has so much reserve as James Bond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:05.820
that he makes Sean Connery seem&nbsp;
almost frenetic by comparison."

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:09.180
Reviews in the UK were just as&nbsp;
critical of the leading man's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:13.380
turn as the British Secret Service&nbsp;
agent, with London-based outlet Time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:19.320
Out calling him a "stunning nonentity"&nbsp;
fronting a film with "no redeeming features."

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:24.360
1981's The Clash of the Titans attempted to&nbsp;
give Greek mythology the Star Wars treatment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:29.760
taking the story of Perseus and turning it into&nbsp;
a sprawling epic. Director Desmond Davis went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:35.280
with Harry Hamlin in the role of Perseus, a son&nbsp;
of Zeus who in legend beheads the Gorgon Medusa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:40.860
in order to save his beloved Princess Andromeda&nbsp;
from a giant Kraken. Hamlin remains active in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:45.000
the industry, though he's still arguably&nbsp;
best remembered for his turn as Perseus.

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:49.620
The same cannot be said for Sam Worthington,&nbsp;
best known as the star of James Cameron's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:54.180
budding Avatar franchise. Director Louis&nbsp;
Leterrier entrusted the Australian with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:58.920
the lead role in his 2010 remake, though his&nbsp;
choice was called into question when the negative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:02.880
reviews started to pour in. Worthington's&nbsp;
earned his share of acclaim over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:07.800
but the Titans remake is one release he most&nbsp;
likely wouldn't mind leaving off his résumé;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:13.020
Quickflix's Simon Miraudo questioned how&nbsp;
Worthington had ever managed to become a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:16.440
Hollywood actor after watching his&nbsp;
performance as Perseus, in which

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:19.380
"[He] does nothing but glare. His battle speeches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:22.320
are neither rousing nor&nbsp;
particularly intelligible."

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:27.720
Despite its name, 1984's Friday the&nbsp;
13th Part 4: The Final Chapter was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:31.740
far from the last installment in this&nbsp;
long-running horror franchise. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:35.760
eight more films have been made since the&nbsp;
so-called final chapter, though the one that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:40.860
directly followed it marked a major dropoff&nbsp;
in quality. Part 4 has been described as,

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:46.140
"One of the last really excellent horror romps&nbsp;
that focus on character dynamic and family."

00:12:46.140 --> 00:12:50.220
Felix Vasquez of Cinema Crazed&nbsp;
praised Corey Feldman for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:52.440
"standout performance as young Tommy Jarvis."

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:56.820
Feldman went on to make a name for himself in&nbsp;
The Goonies, Stand by Me, and The Lost Boys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.820 --> 00:13:01.740
though before he did, he reprised his&nbsp;
Friday the 13th role in 1985's Part 5:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:07.140
A New Beginning. Unfortunately, he only appeared&nbsp;
in the prologue, and a time jump meant he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:12.360
replaced by the painfully dull John Shepherd.&nbsp;
Teenage Tommy was static compared to his younger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:16.740
self, though Shepherd shouldn't shoulder the&nbsp;
blame entirely. Some blamed what they called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:21.840
the "hamfisted directing" of David Steinmann&nbsp;
while James Kendrick of Q Network argued that

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:27.000
"[The] gory antics are so restrained by the&nbsp;
insistence of the MPAA rating system that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:31.935
the film feels like it was edited with&nbsp;
one of Jason's bloody-rusty weapons."

00:13:31.935 --> 00:13:37.800
Veteran film critic Mark Kermode summed up 2015's&nbsp;
The Transporter Refueled with a single question:

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:42.060
"What exactly is the point of a&nbsp;
'Transporter' movie without Jason Statham?"

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:45.540
When it was revealed that Ed Skrein&nbsp;
was to replace Statham as Special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:49.800
Forces op-turned-private driver Frank&nbsp;
Martin in the fourth Transporter film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:53.580
questions were raised about the former's&nbsp;
ability to carry the franchise forward.

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:58.740
Though the Transporter films didn't exactly change&nbsp;
the action movie landscape, they benefited from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.740 --> 00:14:03.720
Statham's presence, athletic prowess, and combat&nbsp;
skills. His replacement lacked that experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:08.220
and it showed. Skrein began taking classes&nbsp;
in various martial arts disciplines as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:13.500
prepared for both The Transporter Refueled&nbsp;
and his role as Ajax in Deadpool, but even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:18.180
he acknowledged that he was way behind many of&nbsp;
his peers in that department. He told the BBC,

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:20.820
"I'm very respectful and realize I'm not going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.820 --> 00:14:23.760
be able to do things meant for&nbsp;
10 or 20 years of training."

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:27.480
The action in The Transporter Refueled&nbsp;
was ultimately limited by the new star's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:31.800
fledgling abilities. Ed Skrein's one&nbsp;
and only outing as Frank Martin was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:36.792
commercial and critical flop that left the Luc&nbsp;
Besson-produced franchise dead in the water.

00:14:37.140 --> 00:14:42.000
Rooney Mara took on the role of androgynous&nbsp;
computer hacker Lisbeth Salander starring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:47.640
opposite Daniel Craig in 2011's The Girl with&nbsp;
the Dragon Tattoo. When the 2018 sequel The Girl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:52.500
in the Spider's Web was announced, fans of David&nbsp;
Fincher's critically acclaimed first film were a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:57.267
little disappointed that both Mara and Craig had&nbsp;
been recast, and so was Mara. She told Yahoo!,

00:14:57.267 --> 00:15:01.260
"I was hopeful to come back, but&nbsp;
they decided to do a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:05.160
thing. It was always a character I wanted&nbsp;
to revisit, but that's not what happened."

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:09.240
Sverrir Gudnason replaced Daniel Craig,&nbsp;
while Claire Foy stepped into the pale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:13.440
pierced skin of Salander. Unfortunately, she&nbsp;
didn't look comfortable in it. At the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:17.940
she was receiving acclaim for her turn as&nbsp;
the young Queen Elizabeth II in The Crown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:22.080
but Foy couldn't pull off Salander's&nbsp;
cold eccentricities with the same poise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.080 --> 00:15:26.460
as her predecessor. Critics were by and large&nbsp;
unimpressed with her version of the goth hacker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.460 --> 00:15:30.780
with many questioning why she was chosen&nbsp;
for the part. The Observer's Wendy Ide said,

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:35.040
"Much as I admire her, Foy is miscast&nbsp;
here. She’s just too empathetic for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:38.100
role that requires an almost&nbsp;
reptilian lack of emotion."

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:43.860
IndieWire's senior film critic David Ehrlich&nbsp;
called her both "terrible [and] terribly miscast."

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:50.580
The first RoboCop stars Peter Weller as the&nbsp;
titular cyborg. He roams a dystopian crime-ridden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.580 --> 00:15:55.320
version of Detroit, mercilessly dealing with&nbsp;
lawbreakers. Weller reprised the role in RoboCop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:59.580
2 but turned down the chance to play him again&nbsp;
in a third movie. Producers needed a replacement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.580 --> 00:16:04.500
and their hearts were apparently set on a&nbsp;
little-known actor named Robert John Burke.

00:16:04.500 --> 00:16:06.720
In a 2015 interview, Burke said,

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:10.440
"In God's great earth they thought I&nbsp;
was the only actor who could do it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:15.540
I don't know why. I think physically I poked&nbsp;
like Peter Weller, and I had also had extensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.540 --> 00:16:19.440
karate training, pantomime training,&nbsp;
and body training, and I fit the suit."

00:16:19.440 --> 00:16:24.180
Unsurprisingly, handing the lead role over to&nbsp;
the relatively inexperienced Burke just because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:28.620
he kind of resembled Weller didn't work out very&nbsp;
well. He may have looked like him in the suit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:31.860
but as the New York Times said in&nbsp;
its review of the box office bomb,

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:36.660
"[Burke is] lacking his forerunner's&nbsp;
tongue-in-cheek glint of authoritarian machismo."

00:16:36.660 --> 00:16:38.100
"You have the right to an attorney."

00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:40.680
"What is thiiiiiis?"

00:16:40.680 --> 00:16:46.200
From his delivery to the way he moved, Burke just&nbsp;
wasn't as convincing as RoboCop. As Empire noted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:49.392
he appeared to be "a lot less&nbsp;
comfortable with the hardware."

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:56.100
1988's Big is often cited as Tom Hanks' breakout&nbsp;
movie, but he actually announced himself as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.100 --> 00:17:01.140
potential star some four years earlier in&nbsp;
Ron Howard's fantasy rom-com Splash. Hanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:06.060
plays Allen Bauer, a young man who returns to&nbsp;
the spot of what he believes was a near-death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.060 --> 00:17:10.200
hallucination to discover that the woman he&nbsp;
saw in the water the day he almost drowned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:15.360
is indeed real. Allen falls for mermaid Madison&nbsp;
and ditches his life on land to live under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.360 --> 00:17:19.500
sea with her in a goofy yet undeniably&nbsp;
feel-good ending. It's a beloved movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.500 --> 00:17:24.120
though the same cannot be said about the&nbsp;
obscure, made-for-TV sequel, Splash, Too.

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:29.580
Helmed by writer, director, and actor Greg&nbsp;
Antonacci, the highly forgettable and completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.580 --> 00:17:34.860
unnecessary Splash, Too brought Allen and Madison&nbsp;
back to dry land, totally ignoring the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:39.420
the government wanted to lock her up in a lab&nbsp;
in the previous installment. Todd Waring took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:44.040
over as Allen Bauer, but despite his best&nbsp;
efforts, he lacked the everyman charm and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.040 --> 00:17:48.480
comedic timing that was abundant in Hanks.&nbsp;
Film Fad said in its review of the sequel,

00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:52.568
"The less said about the actors replacing&nbsp;
Tom Hanks and John Candy the better."

00:17:52.868 --> 00:17:59.460
Dallas had its fair share of character&nbsp;
replacements during its run from 1978 to 1991,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:05.340
as any show that airs for 14 seasons inevitably&nbsp;
does. But one that still stands out with fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.340 --> 00:18:11.160
to this day is the role of Miss Ellie Ewing.&nbsp;
Barbara Bel Geddes originated the part and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:16.320
played it right up until 1984, when she&nbsp;
left in order to have a quadruple bypass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:21.180
heart surgery. At the time she was replaced&nbsp;
by Donna Reed, and fans were not into it.

00:18:21.180 --> 00:18:26.640
In fact, fans were so not into it that six&nbsp;
months later, when Bel Geddes had recovered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:30.600
she was asked back onto the show.&nbsp;
Reed didn't go down without a fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:34.800
though — she tried to sue and halt&nbsp;
production. In the end, the judge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:39.540
didn't agree with her and the series carried&nbsp;
on. Bel Geddes continued playing Miss Ewing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.540 --> 00:18:45.300
for the remainder of the series, bringing&nbsp;
life to the character for 300 episodes.

00:18:45.300 --> 00:18:51.120
When Suits hit airwaves in 2011, critics had&nbsp;
positive but somewhat mixed reviews on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:57.180
premise, which turned the tired legal drama format&nbsp;
on its head with the introduction of Mike Ross,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.180 --> 00:19:01.440
a wannabe lawyer without a degree, and&nbsp;
Harvey Specter, the hotshot attorney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.440 --> 00:19:06.360
who mentored him. Shot in Toronto, the series&nbsp;
wound up being a massive hit for the network,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.360 --> 00:19:09.540
and was considered the last of its blue-skies era.

00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:12.380
"Hey, you ready for this?"

00:19:12.380 --> 00:19:14.160
"Yeah, I am."

00:19:14.160 --> 00:19:20.760
However, that all ended in 2017, when Patrick&nbsp;
J. Adams and Meghan Markle, who played Mike Ross&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:25.740
and his love interest Rachel Zane respectively,&nbsp;
announced they were leaving the show after seven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.740 --> 00:19:31.800
seasons. At that point another original series&nbsp;
star, Gina Torres, also exited, leaving the cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.800 --> 00:19:38.160
down three members. As a result, the show brought&nbsp;
on Dulé Hill and Katherine Heigl, while upping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:43.740
Amanda Schull from recurring to full-time status&nbsp;
in season 8. However, the new mix of characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:49.020
didn't resonate as well as the old school ones,&nbsp;
and the show wrapped for good in season 9.

00:19:49.620 --> 00:19:54.300
It seems like networks have been trying&nbsp;
to recreate the soapy primetime success of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.300 --> 00:20:00.060
Desperate Housewives ever since that show went&nbsp;
off the air in 2012. ABC thought it came close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:06.180
with the 2013 entry Mistresses, which failed&nbsp;
to earn critical praise but still snagged a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.180 --> 00:20:10.920
second-season renewal following its steamy&nbsp;
first-season summer run. Fans continued to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:15.240
bake in the hotness of it all the following&nbsp;
summer, but when the show was renewed for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.240 --> 00:20:20.100
third season, ABC revealed it was moving&nbsp;
production from Los Angeles to Vancouver.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:25.260
That was bad news for star Alyssa Milano,&nbsp;
who couldn't be that far from her family.

00:20:25.260 --> 00:20:29.280
Milano stepped away as a result, and&nbsp;
the show recruited Jennifer Esposito&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.280 --> 00:20:34.380
to fill that void with her Calista Raines&nbsp;
character. That lasted one season and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.380 --> 00:20:39.180
Esposito also left the series in order&nbsp;
to pursue other projects. Surprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:43.800
the show was renewed for one more season,&nbsp;
but without Milano fans just didn't seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:49.260
that into it and the series was axed for&nbsp;
good after a very dramatic season 4 finale.

00:20:49.260 --> 00:20:55.140
It's no secret that Julie Benz did not want&nbsp;
to exit Dexter. After she was killed off in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.140 --> 00:20:58.800
the show's fourth season, she admitted&nbsp;
to reporters that she, quote, "bawled"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.800 --> 00:21:03.360
and said it was the worst thing to ever happen&nbsp;
to her in her career. It seems as though fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.360 --> 00:21:08.880
felt the same way. While the decision was purely a&nbsp;
creative one, the salt in the wound for passionate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.880 --> 00:21:14.580
viewers came in season 5 when Julia Stiles&nbsp;
was cast as Lumen Pierce, a new love interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.580 --> 00:21:19.920
for Dexter. The character lasted the entire&nbsp;
season, and finally exited ahead of season 6.

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:25.140
For many it was a turning point, especially as&nbsp;
they watched the show go on to cast other guest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:25.140 --> 00:21:30.000
stars, like Colin Hanks and Yvonne&nbsp;
Strahovski, to fill the void. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:35.160
writers eventually went so far as to almost&nbsp;
pair Dexter up with his adoptive sister Deb.

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:40.320
Chemistry is particularly important when&nbsp;
casting a family sitcom, so fans were a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.320 --> 00:21:45.120
concerned when Julia Butters left American&nbsp;
Housewife as the youngest Otto child ahead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:50.160
of its fifth-season premiere. Butters brought a&nbsp;
notable charm to the character of Anna-Kat Otto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.160 --> 00:21:56.340
so much so that when Quentin Tarantino saw her on&nbsp;
his screen he cast her as serious young actress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:56.340 --> 00:22:02.220
Trudi in Once Upon A Time in Hollywood. That gig&nbsp;
wound up being a game-changer for the young actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.220 --> 00:22:07.560
because all kinds of subsequent offers came&nbsp;
in and she left the ABC series to pursue them.

00:22:07.560 --> 00:22:12.420
In turn, American Housewife snatched&nbsp;
up Giselle Eisenberg, fresh from her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.420 --> 00:22:18.180
four-season run on the CBS sitcom Life in&nbsp;
Pieces. While Eisenberg is undeniably talented,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:18.180 --> 00:22:23.460
it's been hard for fans to adapt to the&nbsp;
change following the show's return in 2020.

00:22:24.300 --> 00:22:26.334
"Yeah...kids change."

00:22:26.334 --> 00:22:31.740
It's no secret that the children on Tim Allen's&nbsp;
Last Man Standing changed pretty drastically over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.740 --> 00:22:36.420
the show's run. Amanda Fuller joined the&nbsp;
project in season two as Kristin Baxter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.420 --> 00:22:40.920
replacing Alexandra Krosney, who&nbsp;
originated the role. Then there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.920 --> 00:22:45.540
the fact that four different actors have&nbsp;
played the part of Kristin's son Boyd as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:45.540 --> 00:22:49.680
the character has aged over the years.&nbsp;
But it was Molly Ephraim's exit from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.680 --> 00:22:54.360
series following season six that really&nbsp;
threw Last Man Standing fans for a loop.

00:22:54.360 --> 00:23:00.000
As middle child Mandy, Ephraim had her share of&nbsp;
meaty storylines that made her a show fixture.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:06.360
But when ABC canceled Last Man Standing in 2017&nbsp;
and Fox revived it for a seventh-season run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.360 --> 00:23:09.540
Ephraim didn't go with it. She&nbsp;
had signed up for new projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.540 --> 00:23:14.820
and was unable to return. Molly McCook was cast&nbsp;
in her place, and although the series lasted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:14.820 --> 00:23:21.421
three seasons on Fox before its cancelation in&nbsp;
2021, the character was just never the same.

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:27.300
Now this is a story all about how the casting&nbsp;
on Fresh Prince got turned upside down. Fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:27.300 --> 00:23:31.860
everywhere are familiar with the behind-the-scenes&nbsp;
strife that went down between Will Smith and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:31.860 --> 00:23:37.800
original Aunt Viv actress Janet Hubert, who played&nbsp;
the role for three notable seasons before refusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.800 --> 00:23:43.140
to renew her contract under what she considered&nbsp;
to be a bad deal. As a result she exited the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.140 --> 00:23:48.600
and she was replaced by Daphne Maxwell Reid — an&nbsp;
actor who was originally considered for the role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:48.600 --> 00:23:53.520
but refused to audition because a TV show starring&nbsp;
a young rapper didn't sound like her thing.

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:56.340
"There are a lot of sick,&nbsp;
demented perverts out there."&nbsp;

00:23:56.880 --> 00:23:58.200
"Greetings, all."

00:23:58.200 --> 00:24:04.200
Although The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air went on&nbsp;
for three more seasons with the new Aunt Viv,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.200 --> 00:24:08.640
there was no replacing Hubert's comedic&nbsp;
timing. Later, when Smith got the gang&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:08.640 --> 00:24:13.200
back together for the 2020 reunion, he&nbsp;
and Hubert sat down for the first time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:18.660
to finally iron things out. The result was the&nbsp;
two Aunt Vivs meeting for the very first time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.660 --> 00:24:22.980
as many fans wondered what the show could&nbsp;
have been had Hubert stayed on after all.

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:29.700
When the sixth season of this comedy kicked off&nbsp;
in 1969, viewers were left twitching harder than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:29.700 --> 00:24:35.460
Samantha and her magical nose when Emmy nominee&nbsp;
Dick York was suddenly gone as Darrin Stephens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:35.460 --> 00:24:41.760
In his place was Dick Sargent, and fan speculation&nbsp;
as to what happened kicked into overdrive. As it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:45.840
turned out, York had previously injured his&nbsp;
back pretty badly while shooting a movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:45.840 --> 00:24:50.160
and the pain led him down a dark path&nbsp;
of prescription pills. Eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:54.120
he realized he had to leave the show,&nbsp;
and his career never really recovered.

00:24:54.120 --> 00:25:00.960
As for Sargent? He stayed in the role until the&nbsp;
series finale in 1972. Many fans felt the series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:00.960 --> 00:25:05.760
wasn't the same, though, and it's worth noting&nbsp;
that although Sargent won a Most Irreplaceable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:05.760 --> 00:25:12.120
Replacement TV Land Award in 2006, he was never&nbsp;
nominated for an Emmy Award like his predecessor.

00:25:12.900 --> 00:25:17.940
Have you heard the one about the two Beckys? When&nbsp;
Roseanne kicked off its first iteration back in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:17.940 --> 00:25:24.300
1988, it was actor Lecy Goranson who originated&nbsp;
the role of Becky, the eldest child to Dan and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:24.300 --> 00:25:29.820
Roseanne. Fast forward to season five, though, and&nbsp;
Goranson had enrolled in college, and as a result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:29.820 --> 00:25:35.580
she was only available to film part-time, usually&nbsp;
for a phone conversation. The following year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:35.580 --> 00:25:40.440
producers wanted Becky back in a more permanent&nbsp;
role, so they recast her with Sarah Chalke.

00:25:40.440 --> 00:25:46.020
That was fine, but things got complicated&nbsp;
when Goranson briefly returned in Season 8&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:46.020 --> 00:25:49.860
after finishing school. The following&nbsp;
year Chalke was back in the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:54.720
and "the two Beckys" became a running thing&nbsp;
that frustrated some viewers. To their relief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:54.720 --> 00:26:00.180
both actors were asked back for the Roseanne&nbsp;
revival in 2018, but only Goranson returned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:00.180 --> 00:26:04.440
to play Becky. Producers hired Chalke to&nbsp;
instead play a character named Andrea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:08.801
who asked Becky to be her surrogate in&nbsp;
a clever wink to the whole situation.

00:26:09.420 --> 00:26:14.040
For five and a half seasons, Nashville fans&nbsp;
followed Connie Britton's superstar character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:14.040 --> 00:26:18.900
Rayna Jaymes, as she balanced motherhood,&nbsp;
singing, and reconnecting with her one true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:18.900 --> 00:26:24.060
love, Deacon Claybourne. But that all came&nbsp;
crashing down when Britton shocked fans and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:30.240
exited the series in 2017 with a devastating&nbsp;
storyline that left her family all alone.

00:26:30.240 --> 00:26:34.800
It turned out Britton was ready for a change,&nbsp;
but she was having a hard time letting go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:34.800 --> 00:26:39.900
of the people she worked with. So when CMT&nbsp;
revived Nashville for a fifth season after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:39.900 --> 00:26:45.240
ABC axed it at the end of its run in 2016,&nbsp;
Britton decided it was finally time to move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:45.240 --> 00:26:49.920
on. After the fictional singer's death there&nbsp;
were plenty of other characters to root for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:49.920 --> 00:26:54.900
and the show may have ultimately survived. But&nbsp;
then the writers introduced Kaitlin Doubleday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.900 --> 00:27:00.060
as Jessie Caine, a new love interest for&nbsp;
Deacon, and loyal fans were definitely not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:00.060 --> 00:27:05.160
happy. The series took a bit of a dive, and&nbsp;
season 6 ultimately wound up being the last.

00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:10.200
Throughout its eight-season run, House&nbsp;
had a history of replacing characters to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:10.200 --> 00:27:14.580
switch up the storyline and keep things&nbsp;
fresh, but Season 7 is when the casting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:14.580 --> 00:27:19.080
situation really got out of control. That&nbsp;
year, Olivia Wilde took a leave of absence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:19.080 --> 00:27:24.600
from her fan-favorite gig as Thirteen to&nbsp;
film Cowboys &amp; Aliens, while original actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:24.600 --> 00:27:29.700
Lisa Edelstein announced her departure as&nbsp;
Dr. Lisa Cuddy. Considering the shakeups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.700 --> 00:27:34.320
it seemed pretty clear to longtime fans that&nbsp;
the series was nearing its expiration date.

00:27:34.320 --> 00:27:39.000
To try and inject new life into the&nbsp;
show, producers hired Odette Annable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:43.320
and Charlyne Yi to play the latest&nbsp;
fellows alongside Amber Tamblyn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:43.320 --> 00:27:47.580
but the new crew never quite lived up&nbsp;
to the original cast, and as season 8&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.580 --> 00:27:51.060
was in progress, Fox announced that&nbsp;
it would also be the show's last.

00:27:51.960 --> 00:27:58.140
Today Beverly Hills 90210 is also known as&nbsp;
TV's most famous zip code. But before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:58.140 --> 00:28:03.540
show earned that status, its original cast had to&nbsp;
win over primetime fans with its older actors and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.540 --> 00:28:09.000
soapy storylines. Enter Jason Priestley and&nbsp;
Shannen Doherty as the famous Walsh twins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:13.440
Brandon and Brenda. From the start, fans&nbsp;
were into their chemistry with the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:13.440 --> 00:28:18.360
other original cast members, but behind the&nbsp;
scenes it was a different story. Reports of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:18.360 --> 00:28:23.820
on-set tension between Doherty and the rest of&nbsp;
the cast — specifically Jenie Garth — surfaced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:23.820 --> 00:28:27.420
and in 1994, Doherty was&nbsp;
axed from the show for good.

00:28:27.420 --> 00:28:33.300
That year she was replaced by Tiffani Thiessen,&nbsp;
who had her own fanbase from her days as Kelly on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:33.300 --> 00:28:37.200
Saved by the Bell. But not all fans were&nbsp;
into the new character, Valerie Malone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:37.200 --> 00:28:42.480
a family friend of the Walshes. It was clear&nbsp;
Val was meant to replace Brenda, but Saved by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:42.480 --> 00:28:47.040
the Bell fans weren't sure how to receive this&nbsp;
new, adult version of the actor. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:47.040 --> 00:28:51.420
the character had her moments, but she was no&nbsp;
Brenda, and the tone of the series changed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:51.420 --> 00:28:58.020
her casting. She stayed on for most of the rest of&nbsp;
the series, but to this day you can ask any 90210&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:58.020 --> 00:29:03.960
fan which character they relate to most, and you&nbsp;
probably won't find many Valerie stans out there.

00:29:03.960 --> 00:29:08.280
Steve Carell was a well-known comic&nbsp;
force from The Daily Show and Anchorman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:08.280 --> 00:29:12.180
before he got his biggest break with the&nbsp;
small screen comedy series The Office.

00:29:12.180 --> 00:29:17.040
The show was an American adaptation of&nbsp;
Ricky Gervais' BBC sitcom of the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:17.040 --> 00:29:19.800
name and became wildly popular with audiences.

00:29:19.800 --> 00:29:24.480
Carell's film career started to take off&nbsp;
shortly thereafter, with starring roles in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:24.480 --> 00:29:29.100
movies like The 40 Year-Old Virgin, Little Miss&nbsp;
Sunshine, and Crazy Stupid Love, elevating him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:29.100 --> 00:29:33.780
to household name status. So, in 2013, when&nbsp;
Carell's contract with The Office was up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:33.780 --> 00:29:38.940
he opted to walk away from the series even though&nbsp;
it was still a critical and ratings hit for NBC.

00:29:38.940 --> 00:29:44.460
The awkward, lovelorn Michael Scott finally&nbsp;
found love with HR director Holly, and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:44.460 --> 00:29:50.160
moved away to Colorado. But Dunder Mifflin still&nbsp;
needed a boss, and that came in the form of oily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:50.160 --> 00:29:54.540
unsettling Robert California,&nbsp;
CEO of its parent company Sabre.

00:29:54.734 --> 00:29:55.814
"Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:56.340 --> 00:30:01.860
I will not be blackmailed by some ineffectual,&nbsp;
privileged, effete, soft penis debutante"

00:30:01.860 --> 00:30:06.540
And it was all downhill from there. The show's&nbsp;
critical favor and viewership numbers took a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:06.540 --> 00:30:11.280
nosedive, and the series finally came to&nbsp;
a close after two Carell-free seasons. 
 &nbsp;

00:30:11.280 --> 00:30:17.400
For its first six seasons, The X-Files was a&nbsp;
huge hit for Fox and made its on-screen pair of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:17.400 --> 00:30:22.560
paranormal investigators international stars. But&nbsp;
prior to the start of the series' seventh season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:22.560 --> 00:30:27.300
David Duchovny sued the network, claiming&nbsp;
that it had sold rights to reruns of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:27.300 --> 00:30:31.260
show at a discount to its own affiliates&nbsp;
and cheated the stars out of profits.

00:30:31.260 --> 00:30:36.600
While the lawsuit was eventually settled, it was&nbsp;
filed right when Duchovny was negotiating working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:36.600 --> 00:30:41.100
on future seasons of the show, and neither he&nbsp;
nor Fox were keen to continue working together.

00:30:41.100 --> 00:30:46.860
So, a deal was reached: Duchovny's character,&nbsp;
Fox Mulder would mysteriously disappear at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:46.860 --> 00:30:51.780
end of Season 7, and would only appear in a&nbsp;
handful of episodes in Season 8. But Gillian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:51.780 --> 00:30:56.640
Anderson's Agent Scully still needed someone to&nbsp;
work with in his absence, so producers auditioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:56.640 --> 00:31:02.940
dozens of actors and eventually settled on Robert&nbsp;
Patrick to come in as FBI agent John Doggett.

00:31:02.940 --> 00:31:06.480
Another actress was also hired on&nbsp;
in case Anderson decided to leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:06.480 --> 00:31:09.360
the show after her contract ended,&nbsp;
too. It didn't much matter, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:09.360 --> 00:31:12.540
because without Duchovny in his&nbsp;
regular role, viewers tuned out.

00:31:12.540 --> 00:31:17.700
"You saved me. As difficult&nbsp;
and as frustrating as it’s been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:21.660
sometimes, your g–damn strict rationalism and&nbsp;
science has saved me a thousand times over."
 &nbsp;

00:31:22.560 --> 00:31:26.400
This ABC sitcom was forced to&nbsp;
reformat after the unexpected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:26.400 --> 00:31:30.780
death of its star. 8 Simple Rules was&nbsp;
built around TV legend John Ritter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:30.780 --> 00:31:35.880
who starred as cranky suburban dad desperately&nbsp;
trying to rein in his wild teenage children.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:35.880 --> 00:31:41.280
Filming had just begun on the second season of&nbsp;
the series when Ritter fell ill on the set and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:41.280 --> 00:31:46.140
sought medical treatment. He died later that day&nbsp;
of a heart defect that had never been diagnosed.

00:31:46.140 --> 00:31:49.440
ABC announced that the show&nbsp;
would go on — and it did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:49.440 --> 00:31:53.220
with some necessary changes. No&nbsp;
longer a show about a cranky dad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:53.220 --> 00:31:58.440
it became one about a family trying to pick up&nbsp;
the pieces after the death of its patriarch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:58.440 --> 00:32:03.540
Two new stars joined the cast to fill the&nbsp;
void left by Ritter: cousin C.J. and Grandpa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:08.460
Jim. But without Ritter, the show only lasted&nbsp;
another season before being shelved for good.

00:32:08.460 --> 00:32:15.120
Actor/musician Steve Burns beat out roughly&nbsp;
1,000 other actors in 1996 for the only human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:15.120 --> 00:32:19.440
role on Blue's Clues, Nickelodeon's&nbsp;
interactive, animation/live action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:19.440 --> 00:32:24.540
hybrid show for preschoolers. Burns played&nbsp;
the striped-shirted host and owner of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:24.540 --> 00:32:28.860
cartoon dog named Blue — he'd talk directly&nbsp;
to the young viewers at home, urging them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:28.860 --> 00:32:32.820
to help him solve each episode's riddle or&nbsp;
mystery by yelling things at their screens.

00:32:32.820 --> 00:32:37.800
In 2002, after about 100 episodes,&nbsp;
Burns decided it was time to leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:37.800 --> 00:32:41.160
the series. He wanted to take on&nbsp;
other roles as well as perform music.

00:32:41.160 --> 00:32:47.280
Nickelodeon hired actor Donovan Patton to be the&nbsp;
new host of Blue's Clues, playing Steve's younger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:47.280 --> 00:32:52.680
brother Joe. So as not to traumatize or confuse&nbsp;
the show's very young viewers, Steve carefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:52.680 --> 00:32:58.920
explained that he was leaving for college, and&nbsp;
Joe would be looking after Blue. Patton as Joe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:58.920 --> 00:33:04.020
proved to be the gentle and able host Blue's&nbsp;
Clues needed, but he just couldn't replace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:04.020 --> 00:33:09.180
someone so closely associated with the role to&nbsp;
an army of toddlers. After around 50 episodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:09.180 --> 00:33:14.880
with Patton as Joe, Blue's Clues was canceled.
 
This comedy about medical interns desperately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:14.880 --> 00:33:18.180
trying to figure out what they were&nbsp;
doing as they worked in a hospital was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:18.180 --> 00:33:22.620
a critically-acclaimed, Emmy-nominated&nbsp;
hit over its first few seasons on NBC.

00:33:22.620 --> 00:33:27.540
The network dropped the show after seven&nbsp;
seasons, only to see ABC pick it up. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:27.540 --> 00:33:31.560
show's writers and cast prepared a true&nbsp;
series finale at the end of the year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:31.560 --> 00:33:36.540
only for ABC to unexpectedly renew&nbsp;
the series again for season 9.

00:33:36.540 --> 00:33:40.980
But star Zach Braff, the focus of&nbsp;
the show as young doctor John Dorian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:40.980 --> 00:33:42.960
was ready to move on to other things.

00:33:43.147 --> 00:33:44.367
"Onward! Yee-haw!"

00:33:46.680 --> 00:33:49.334
"Oh good god."

00:33:49.334 --> 00:33:53.700
So the show was retooled and rebooted, no&nbsp;
longer taking place at a teaching hospital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:53.700 --> 00:33:59.160
but at a medical school. Doctor Cox and Doctor&nbsp;
Turk were now teachers instead of active surgeons.

00:33:59.334 --> 00:34:01.560
"I'm just back to teach some classes."

00:34:01.560 --> 00:34:06.360
Replacing Braff as the center of the&nbsp;
show and narrator was Dr. Lucy Bennett,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:06.360 --> 00:34:11.520
but the big changes were too much to take, and&nbsp;
the ninth season of Scrubs was also its last.
 &nbsp;

00:34:11.520 --> 00:34:16.200
Michael J. Fox returned to regular series&nbsp;
television for the first time since the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:16.200 --> 00:34:22.020
of Family Ties with Spin City. On the ABC sitcom,&nbsp;
he played Mike Flaherty, a wheeler-dealer for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:22.020 --> 00:34:27.180
New York City deputy mayor. Two years later, Fox&nbsp;
announced that he had been battling Parkinson's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:27.180 --> 00:34:31.800
disease and left the show to spend more time&nbsp;
raising funds for research on the disease.

00:34:31.800 --> 00:34:37.200
Replacing him as deputy mayor was a guy named&nbsp;
Charlie Crawford, portrayed by Charlie Sheen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:37.200 --> 00:34:42.420
With Fox out, the show became more of a romantic&nbsp;
comedy surrounding Sheen and Heather Locklear's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:42.420 --> 00:34:48.000
characters, but viewership tanked shortly after&nbsp;
the replacement, so ABC cut the cord on Spin City.

00:34:48.134 --> 00:34:51.034
"I guess you guys aren’t ready for that yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:52.800 --> 00:34:57.360
But your kids are gonna love it."
 
Delta Burke had not been happy on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:57.360 --> 00:35:01.800
the set of Designing Women for years. She even&nbsp;
claimed that a producer routinely screamed at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:01.800 --> 00:35:05.760
cast members and told them they were expendable&nbsp;
and criticized Burke when she gained weight.

00:35:06.008 --> 00:35:07.328
"Are you serious?!"

00:35:07.328 --> 00:35:12.060
CBS grew so tired of Burke publicly&nbsp;
trashing the show's producers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:12.060 --> 00:35:14.640
the network in the press that&nbsp;
they fired her from the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:14.640 --> 00:35:18.240
and co-star Jean Smart followed her&nbsp;
out the door shortly thereafter.

00:35:18.866 --> 00:35:22.249
"Every time I look over at the&nbsp;
sofa I kinda miss seeing her."

00:35:22.249 --> 00:35:25.740
That meant that Designing Women&nbsp;
had lost half of its main cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:25.740 --> 00:35:29.340
but the show was still a ratings draw, so&nbsp;
the network brought in some replacements.

00:35:29.340 --> 00:35:33.480
"Now I know that all of our nerves are a&nbsp;
little bit jangly because of this silly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:33.480 --> 00:35:38.935
old money situation but you know what we&nbsp;
gotta do? We've got to look for the light."

00:35:38.935 --> 00:35:43.560
Julia Duffy arrived as Suzanne's cousin,&nbsp;
and Jan Hooks came in as Carlene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:43.560 --> 00:35:48.900
Charlene's sister. Despite ratings on par&nbsp;
with previous years, CBS fired Duffy after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:48.900 --> 00:35:53.940
her sole season on the series, and replaced&nbsp;
her with Judith Ivey as a rich Texan widow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:53.940 --> 00:35:59.220
named B.J. Poteet. It didn't do much good,&nbsp;
because CBS moved the show to Friday nights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:59.220 --> 00:36:02.668
where it slipped into ratings&nbsp;
oblivion — and then cancellation.

00:36:02.668 --> 00:36:09.534
"And that… Is the night the&nbsp;
lights went out in Georgia"

00:36:09.534 --> 00:36:15.900
After seven seasons of riding on a high — ratings&nbsp;
wise, at least — two of That 70s Show's main stars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:15.900 --> 00:36:20.280
decided to step away from the series. Topher&nbsp;
Grace decided to leave the show in order to more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:20.280 --> 00:36:24.540
fiercely pursue his blossoming film career and&nbsp;
was written out of the eighth season altogether.

00:36:24.540 --> 00:36:29.220
Meanwhile, Ashton Kutcher decided his&nbsp;
time in the bell bottoms was done too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:29.220 --> 00:36:32.100
but did appear for a few of&nbsp;
Season 8's early episodes.

00:36:32.100 --> 00:36:38.040
To replace two very big holes in its cast,&nbsp;
producers hired Mad TV veteran Josh Meyers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:38.040 --> 00:36:43.740
His character, Randy, joins Hyde's record store&nbsp;
Grooves and has a brief relationship with Donna.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:43.740 --> 00:36:48.600
But Randy didn't have much time to mesh with&nbsp;
the ensemble because the decimated cast led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:48.600 --> 00:36:52.560
to diminished popularity for the show, and it&nbsp;
was ultimately canned at the end of the season.

00:36:52.560 --> 00:36:57.382
"You have the right to remain BURNED."

00:36:57.660 --> 00:37:02.340
Even though Community never had a huge&nbsp;
audience during its five-year run on NBC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:02.340 --> 00:37:05.820
it was still a critically-acclaimed&nbsp;
comedy with a passionate cult following.

00:37:05.820 --> 00:37:11.940
Five seasons was ultimately NBC's limit, but&nbsp;
internet giant Yahoo! stepped in and renewed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:11.940 --> 00:37:16.500
Community for a sixth season, intending to&nbsp;
position the comedy as a premiere offering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:16.500 --> 00:37:22.020
on its streaming service Yahoo! Screen. Original&nbsp;
regulars Joel McHale, Gillian Jacobs, Danny Pudi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:22.020 --> 00:37:27.240
Ken Jeong, and Alison Brie followed the series&nbsp;
to Yahoo!, but the show had already been losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:27.240 --> 00:37:31.140
cast members and left and right, and kept&nbsp;
losing them as it transitioned to streaming.

00:37:31.140 --> 00:37:34.740
Chevy Chase, Donald Glover, and&nbsp;
Yvette Nicole Brown were not part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:34.740 --> 00:37:38.760
of the regular cast of Community's&nbsp;
sixth season. New actors were added,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:38.760 --> 00:37:42.540
including Keith David and Paget&nbsp;
Brewster. But it just didn't work out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:42.540 --> 00:37:48.000
and there was no seventh season of Community on&nbsp;
Yahoo! Screen — or anywhere. Not enough people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:48.000 --> 00:37:53.160
tuned in and the show didn't generate as much&nbsp;
advertising revenue as Yahoo! had hoped. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:53.160 --> 00:37:57.480
revenue was so small that it effectively led&nbsp;
to the complete shutdown of Yahoo! Screen.

00:37:58.140 --> 00:38:00.660
"Show may be canceled and moved to the&nbsp;
internet where it turns out tens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:00.660 --> 00:38:03.420
millions were watching the whole time. May&nbsp;
not matter. Fake commercial may end with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:03.420 --> 00:38:06.600
disclaimer gag which may descend into vain&nbsp;
Chuck Lorre-esque rant by narcissistic creator"

00:38:06.600 --> 00:38:09.480
Sleepy Hollow, Fox's supernatural police drama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:09.480 --> 00:38:13.740
was always a little bit different than other cop&nbsp;
shows. For starters, one of its main characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:13.740 --> 00:38:18.360
was Ichabod Crane from Washington Irving's&nbsp;
short story "The Legend of Sleepy Hollow,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:18.360 --> 00:38:22.680
who comes back to life in the present day&nbsp;
after having died 200 or so years prior.

00:38:22.680 --> 00:38:27.420
Crane then helps fight supernatural menaces&nbsp;
alongside police lieutenant Abbie Mills. Over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:27.420 --> 00:38:31.560
three seasons, Sleepy Hollow settled into&nbsp;
its role as a quirky police procedural,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:31.560 --> 00:38:36.480
and then it did the unthinkable — Abbie died&nbsp;
and actress Nicole Beharie left the series.

00:38:36.480 --> 00:38:40.740
Despite a steep loss of viewers from&nbsp;
season 1 to season 3, Sleepy Hollow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:40.740 --> 00:38:44.760
returned for a fourth season without Abbie.&nbsp;
Ichabod was now partnered with a Homeland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:44.760 --> 00:38:49.560
Security agent named Diana Thomas. The cast&nbsp;
reboot didn't do Sleepy Hollow any favors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:49.560 --> 00:38:52.800
though. The show fell even further&nbsp;
in the ratings and was soon canceled.

00:38:53.940 --> 00:38:57.720
Step by Step was an ABC sitcom&nbsp;
about Carol and Frank Lambert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:57.720 --> 00:39:01.560
two single parents who bring their families&nbsp;
together. In addition to all their kids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:01.560 --> 00:39:06.120
Frank's nephew Cody lived in a van outside&nbsp;
the house. Cody was a good-natured, air-headed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:06.120 --> 00:39:10.440
California dude, basically a sitcom version&nbsp;
of Ted, from Bill &amp; Ted's Excellent Adventure.

00:39:10.440 --> 00:39:11.316
"And I'm Ted Theodore Logan."

00:39:11.316 --> 00:39:13.634
"Yeah!"

00:39:13.634 --> 00:39:19.440
Affable and enthusiastic acting by Sasha Mitchell&nbsp;
made the character a fan favorite. But in 1995,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:19.440 --> 00:39:23.220
when Mitchell was arrested for spousal&nbsp;
assault. He was convicted later that year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:23.220 --> 00:39:27.900
and sentenced to three years of probation&nbsp;
and spousal abuse counseling. Mitchell wound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:27.900 --> 00:39:32.040
up behind bars for violating his parole,&nbsp;
and reportedly attacked his wife again.

00:39:32.040 --> 00:39:35.460
At the beginning of the sixth season&nbsp;
of Step by Step, Cody was gone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:35.460 --> 00:39:39.660
replaced by Jean-Luc, a heavily accented&nbsp;
hairdresser colleague of Carol's. Playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:39.660 --> 00:39:43.020
Jean-Luc was Bronson Pinchot, best&nbsp;
known for playing the excitable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:43.020 --> 00:39:47.820
heavily-accented Balki on Perfect Strangers&nbsp;
and the excitable, heavily-accented Serge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:47.820 --> 00:39:52.800
in Beverly Hills Cop. The new cast addition&nbsp;
didn't work, and ABC canceled Step by Step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:52.800 --> 00:39:57.420
at the end of the season. The show then moved&nbsp;
to CBS for a final year... without Pinchot.

00:39:57.801 --> 00:40:00.240
After the first season of The Facts of Life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:00.240 --> 00:40:05.160
the number of main characters was cut from 10 to&nbsp;
just five: Eastland boarding school housemother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:05.160 --> 00:40:09.540
Mrs. Garrett and four female students. After&nbsp;
those four graduated in the fourth season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:09.540 --> 00:40:14.160
Mrs. Garrett opened a gourmet shop and hired&nbsp;
her former students. And after season seven,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:14.160 --> 00:40:18.540
Mrs. Garrett herself, Charlotte Rae, left&nbsp;
the show. She thought it was time to move on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:18.540 --> 00:40:22.200
later telling Entertainment Weekly that&nbsp;
the show could continue on without her.

00:40:22.200 --> 00:40:27.120
But producers loved the show's dynamic of a wise&nbsp;
older woman doling out advice to young women,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:27.120 --> 00:40:31.500
so they created the role of Mrs. Garrett's&nbsp;
sister Beverly Ann. However, the replacement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:31.500 --> 00:40:36.360
didn't quite work, and by the ninth season, The&nbsp;
Facts of Life was unceremoniously cancelled.

00:40:37.020 --> 00:40:40.800
American Idol has created a number&nbsp;
of stars, such as Kelly Clarkson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:40.800 --> 00:40:45.480
Jennifer Hudson, Adam Lambert, and, of course,&nbsp;
Simon Cowell. Audiences couldn't get enough of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:45.480 --> 00:40:48.840
the judge, who was often brutally and&nbsp;
hilariously honest with contestants.

00:40:48.840 --> 00:40:53.700
"Okay. Best singer in America."

00:40:53.700 --> 00:40:54.413
"Yeah."

00:40:54.413 --> 00:40:57.240
"Right, I can honestly say you&nbsp;
are the worst singer in America."

00:40:57.240 --> 00:41:01.800
Cowell was the biggest star of the series,&nbsp;
which was the most-watched show on TV for an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:01.800 --> 00:41:06.300
unprecedented eight straight seasons. But&nbsp;
in 2010, after nine seasons, he moved on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:06.300 --> 00:41:10.320
in part to develop the American version&nbsp;
of his British talent series The X Factor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:10.320 --> 00:41:14.640
Idol producers had a hard time replacing&nbsp;
Cowell, as well as fellow original judge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:14.640 --> 00:41:19.080
Paula Abdul. The Idol judges' table saw a&nbsp;
lot of stars take a stab at it, but actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:19.080 --> 00:41:23.460
singers just couldn't fill the void left by&nbsp;
the music industry guy who criticizes singers.

00:41:23.460 --> 00:41:24.863
"Maybe we've made a mistake."

00:41:24.863 --> 00:41:27.503
"I think we may have made a mistake."
"We made a big mistake."

00:41:27.708 --> 00:41:33.780
NBC's critically-acclaimed but low-watched comedy&nbsp;
had no plans to replace one of its characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:33.780 --> 00:41:38.700
and best-known star — but tragedy forced the&nbsp;
producers' hand. Between the show's fourth and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:38.700 --> 00:41:43.560
fifth season, star Phil Hartman was shot to death&nbsp;
by his troubled wife. It was a horrific crime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:43.560 --> 00:41:47.400
and a devastating loss for the show. But&nbsp;
in order to complete enough episodes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:47.400 --> 00:41:51.420
sell the show into syndication, NewsRadio&nbsp;
was picked up for another season anyway.

00:41:51.420 --> 00:41:54.660
In the season premiere, it was&nbsp;
explained that Hartman's character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:54.660 --> 00:41:58.920
pompous radio newsreader Bill McNeal,&nbsp;
died of a sudden heart attack. Replacing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:58.920 --> 00:42:03.180
him at the newsreader's desk was a new&nbsp;
character, Max Louis. Weird, insecure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:03.180 --> 00:42:07.560
and wholly unqualified for the position, Max&nbsp;
was portrayed by Hartman's friend and Saturday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:07.560 --> 00:42:11.940
Night Live costar Jon Lovitz. While Lovitz&nbsp;
is a great comic actor, he just couldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:11.940 --> 00:42:16.733
overcome the sadness looming over NewsRadio.&nbsp;
Lovitz's first season would be the show's last.

00:42:17.220 --> 00:42:21.960
For the Fox television adaptation of Lethal&nbsp;
Weapon, Clayne Crawford took over for Mel Gibson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:21.960 --> 00:42:26.640
as "loose cannon" Martin Riggs. And if on-set&nbsp;
reports published by Variety are any indication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:26.640 --> 00:42:31.440
Crawford was even harder to control than his&nbsp;
character. In an episode he directed, a special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:31.440 --> 00:42:36.900
effects explosion went awry, sending a piece&nbsp;
of debris at co-star Damon Wayans. When Wayans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:36.900 --> 00:42:42.060
complained, Crawford referred to his co-star as&nbsp;
a "crybaby." Another time he reportedly screamed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:42.060 --> 00:42:46.260
and swore at a group of children who wandered&nbsp;
near; on yet another occasion, he reportedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:46.260 --> 00:42:50.340
proved so hard to work with that a director&nbsp;
walked off the set in the middle of a shoot.

00:42:50.340 --> 00:42:55.080
In May 2018, after two seasons, producers&nbsp;
eliminated what they thought was the root&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:55.080 --> 00:42:59.220
cause of the show's problems and fired Crawford.&nbsp;
They rushed to find somebody to replace him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:59.220 --> 00:43:03.960
and quickly hired Seann William Scott to play&nbsp;
Wesley Cole, a new partner for Detective Roger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:03.960 --> 00:43:08.400
Murtaugh. Scott couldn't save the show, however.&nbsp;
All that tension didn't totally dissipate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:08.400 --> 00:43:11.880
with Wayans announcing that he planned to&nbsp;
leave the series in the middle of the season.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:11.880 --> 00:43:17.160
Fox decided to wash their hands of the whole&nbsp;
thing in May 2019 and canceled Lethal Weapon.

00:43:17.160 --> 00:43:23.280
From 1998 to 2007, Kevin James and Leah&nbsp;
Remini starred on the popular CBS sitcom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:23.280 --> 00:43:27.780
King of Queens. James would go on to star in a&nbsp;
string of movies like Grown Ups and Paul Blart:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:27.780 --> 00:43:33.600
Mall Cop, but in 2016, he returned to the weekly&nbsp;
CBS sitcom grind with Kevin Can Wait. James&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:33.600 --> 00:43:38.160
portrayed Kevin Gable, a family man who has&nbsp;
to delay retiring from his police job due to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:38.160 --> 00:43:42.540
family and money issues. Playing opposite James&nbsp;
was Erinn Hayes as Kevin's wife, Donna Gable.

00:43:42.540 --> 00:43:46.440
At least, that's how it was for the first&nbsp;
season. Kevin Can Wait producers revamped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:46.440 --> 00:43:50.220
the show for season two: They killed&nbsp;
off Donna Gable and fired Hayes. James&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:50.220 --> 00:43:52.920
explained the decision to the&nbsp;
New York Daily News by saying:

00:43:52.920 --> 00:43:54.900
"We were literally just running out of ideas."

00:43:54.900 --> 00:44:00.480
They also brought in a new face, who was really&nbsp;
a familiar face. Surprisingly, Leah Remini joined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:00.480 --> 00:44:04.980
Kevin Can Wait as Kevin's business partner,&nbsp;
Vanessa. However, the nostalgic retooling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:04.980 --> 00:44:09.120
couldn't overcome the weird behind-the-scenes&nbsp;
shakeup, and ratings actually dropped in season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:09.120 --> 00:44:14.520
two. After the conclusion of the sitcom's second,&nbsp;
ill-fated season, CBS didn't wait to cancel Kevin.

00:44:15.360 --> 00:44:19.920
An ensemble dramedy with a culturally diverse&nbsp;
cast set in an often surreal rural Alaska,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:19.920 --> 00:44:24.660
Northern Exposure was radically different from&nbsp;
most other shows on television. Rob Morrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:24.660 --> 00:44:29.880
starred as Dr. Joel Fleischman, a snobby New York&nbsp;
City doctor who heads north to service the tiny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:29.880 --> 00:44:35.100
town of Cicely. Northern Exposure was a big hit,&nbsp;
reaching the top 20 in the ratings and earning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:35.100 --> 00:44:40.140
four straight Emmy nominations for Outstanding&nbsp;
Drama Series, and winning the prize in 1992.

00:44:40.140 --> 00:44:44.220
But Dr. Fleischman's desire to leave Alaska&nbsp;
never faded, and Morrow wanted to bounce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:44.220 --> 00:44:48.000
too. Halfway through the show's sixth&nbsp;
season, Morrow fled Northern Exposure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:48.000 --> 00:44:52.920
reportedly to pursue new acting challenges and a&nbsp;
movie career. Replacing him was a new physician:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:52.920 --> 00:44:58.500
Dr. Phillip Capra, portrayed by Paul Provenza, a&nbsp;
respected stand-up comedian who had little acting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:58.500 --> 00:45:02.340
experience apart from his work on sitcoms.&nbsp;
That inexperience seems to have showed;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:02.340 --> 00:45:06.960
after just half a season with Provenza at the&nbsp;
helm, the network canceled Northern Exposure.

00:45:07.740 --> 00:45:13.440
The high school sitcom Head of the Class debuted&nbsp;
on ABC in 1986. It explored the relationships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:13.440 --> 00:45:17.400
between a diverse group of highly advanced&nbsp;
Manhattan students and their history teacher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:17.400 --> 00:45:21.480
Charlie Moore, played by Howard Hesseman. A&nbsp;
big part of why Head of the Class worked is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:21.480 --> 00:45:25.680
that it was ensemble-focused; the various&nbsp;
kids had personal and academic problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:25.680 --> 00:45:29.160
and Mr. Moore helped them sort things&nbsp;
out. Hesseman portrayed Moore with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:29.160 --> 00:45:33.000
understatement and subtlety, letting&nbsp;
his younger co-stars take center stage.

00:45:33.000 --> 00:45:36.240
However, the actor hated&nbsp;
Head of the Class. In 1989,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:36.240 --> 00:45:39.720
he told a reporter that he thought the&nbsp;
show didn't have much worth. He said:

00:45:39.720 --> 00:45:42.180
"We're not doing the show that&nbsp;
I was led to believe I'd do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:42.180 --> 00:45:43.800
and it's difficult for me to get off that."

00:45:43.800 --> 00:45:48.780
Unsurprisingly, Hesseman left the show in&nbsp;
1990. In his place was Scottish comedian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:48.780 --> 00:45:53.580
Billy Connolly as a teacher named Billy McGregor.&nbsp;
All of a sudden, a show about high school kids'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:53.580 --> 00:45:59.160
problems was a show about wacky Mr. McGregor.&nbsp;
ABC seemed to realize that, too. In 1991,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:59.160 --> 00:46:03.960
the network canceled Head of the Class and quickly&nbsp;
ordered up a new show called Billy. Connolly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:03.960 --> 00:46:08.460
continued to play McGregor, who was now living as&nbsp;
a boarder in a California single mother's house.

